[19:57:40] STORY-API-001 is complete. Clean build: **189 tests, 0 failures** (170 pre-existing unchanged + 19 new).
[19:57:40] ## What changed
[19:57:40] New `web` package — a Spring Boot HTTP boundary over the existing `application.CraftingProfitService`. Named `web` rather than `api`, since `api` already means the *outbound* GW2 client in this tree. JavaFX is untouched: no view, controller or domain file was modified, and the `spring-boot-maven-plugin` declares no `<executions>`, so `repackage` never runs and the existing jar/JavaFX launch are unaffected.
[19:57:40] `POST /api/crafting/profit` — optional body defaulting to the JavaFX Profit view's own opening settings, three scope kinds onto `repo.DiscChoice`, five validation rules that reject *before* any service is created, and 400/503/500 mappings scoped with `assignableTypes` so the framework's own 404/405 handling stays intact. Transport-only `web.dto` records carry the domain's authoritative values — `totalProfitCopper` copied, never re-derived; blocked reasons preserved; result-less rows reported with `resultAvailable: false` instead of dropped, unlike the JavaFX controller's `continue`.
[19:57:40] `CraftingProfitService` holds the last reload in instance fields, so a shared bean would let concurrent requests observe each other's calculation. The controller takes a `Supplier<CraftingProfitService>` and builds one per request — no extra cost, since the graph cache is re-read per `reload()` on the JavaFX path too.
[19:57:40] ## Verification on the real database
[19:57:40] | | |
[19:57:40] |---|---|
[19:57:40] | First request (cold JVM/caches) | **1981 ms** |
[19:57:40] | Repeats | 942 / 919 / 714 ms |
[19:57:40] | Scale | 3151 rows, 1,306,198 bytes |
[19:57:40] | Application layer alone, same session | 1829 ms first / 937 ms repeat |
[19:57:40] HTTP + mapping + serialization add ~150 ms cold, nothing distinguishable warm. Consistently short, so §23/UD-007's *measured* choice for this endpoint is synchronous — no task or status endpoint was needed. All **3151 rows** verified field-by-field equivalent to the in-process call, across two settings combinations. Also verified by an actual `./mvnw spring-boot:run` answered by `curl` (200/400/200), not just tests.
[19:57:40] ## Three things you should know
[19:57:40] **§33 is not proven by this story, and I've recorded it that way.** 1981 ms is backend request time; the seven-second budget also covers transport of a 1.3 MB response to a browser and rendering 3151 rows, neither of which exists yet. ~5000 ms remains unmeasured until Phase 5. At that size, transport/rendering looks like the likelier risk than backend time.
[19:57:40] **Two dependency versions moved as a consequence of the Spring BOM** — JUnit 5.10.2 → 6.0.3 and Jackson 2 2.17.2 → 2.21.5. The Jackson move wasn't a preference: my first attempt pinned Jackson 2 independently and failed at runtime with `NoClassDefFoundError: com/fasterxml/jackson/annotation/JsonSerializeAs`, because Spring Boot 4 serves HTTP with Jackson 3 and both generations share the annotations artifact. The full suite passes on the new stack and the TestFX sources compile, though no TestFX run was executed — this story changed no UI-observable behavior.
[19:57:40] **The endpoint has no authentication** and serves account-derived data to anything that can reach `server.port`. That matches §20 (TBD, only if required) and is fine for a local single-user setup, but it's worth a decision before any non-local deployment. I flagged it rather than changing it.
[19:57:40] Docs updated: `CURRENT_ARCHITECTURE.md` (§2/§3/§4, new §5.5, §8) and `TEST_STRATEGY.md` (new §35, Layer 6 methodology). `TARGET_ARCHITECTURE.md` deliberately untouched — §30 and §23 are unchanged by this work. No milestone completion declared; Discovery/sync endpoints and the Phase 4 health review remain open.
